@zzak the problem of using always the latest is that you can break compatibility with older Rails versions - for example Rails 6 using latest package intended for Rails 8... :thinking: 

The best solution imho, would be package.json point to the same version as the rails.

Something like:
```json
"dependencies": {
    "turbolinks": "git://github.com/turbolinks/turbolinks.git#main",
// OR
    "turbolinks": "git://github.com/turbolinks/turbolinks.git#v7.0.0"
}
```

reference: https://docs.npmjs.com/cli/v7/configuring-npm/package-json#git-urls-as-dependencies

update: maybe when using '--main' set the github url, then in the other cases use `Rails.version` :eyes:

@ghiculescu This is looking good! I think using `latest` is a good compromise and better than the current behavior, we can always improve.

Would you mind adding a changelog and squashing our commits? 🙇 

Thanks so much for your work on this! 🎉

